1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,740
What should you check when lighting directions

2
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:06,900
or patterns look wrong after stretching a
shape?

3
00:00:07,080 --> 00:00:09,800
First verify normal-tangent perpendicularity
and

4
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:13,140
the inverse-transpose rule in a numerical
example.

5
00:00:13,480 --> 00:00:17,540
Then inspect game wall and roof patterns and
explain UV correspondence,

6
00:00:17,540 --> 00:00:19,260
crop, rotation and flipping.

7
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:21,520
Finally compare repeat and clamp and
demonstrate

8
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:25,340
why wrapping vertices before interpolation
fails.

9
00:00:25,983 --> 00:00:30,303
Normals must remain perpendicular to tangents.

10
00:00:30,443 --> 00:00:34,723
Use column vectors t and n with n transpose t
equal zero.

11
00:00:35,003 --> 00:00:38,863
Let M be the invertible linear transform of
positions and tangents;

12
00:00:39,163 --> 00:00:42,523
do not add translation to directions.

13
00:00:42,803 --> 00:00:45,503
Transform normals with the inverse transpose M
inverse transpose;

14
00:00:45,663 --> 00:00:49,763
inverse and transpose commute in this
expression.

15
00:00:50,163 --> 00:00:55,863
The transformed dot product cancels M inverse
M and equals the

16
00:00:55,983 --> 00:00:59,303
original zero dot product, preserving
perpendicularity.

17
00:00:59,663 --> 00:01:03,703
For orthogonal rotations the inverse transpose
equals M;

18
00:01:03,903 --> 00:01:08,323
positive uniform scale preserves the
normalized direction.

19
00:01:08,643 --> 00:01:13,023
Nonuniform scale and shear require inverse
transpose then normalization;

20
00:01:13,323 --> 00:01:18,503
singular zero-axis transforms have no inverse.

21
00:01:18,663 --> 00:01:22,923
Orientation-reversing transforms also require
consistent front-face handling;

22
00:01:22,923 --> 00:01:27,323
our worked example uses positive scales.

23
00:01:27,967 --> 00:01:32,107
Choose tangent(1,−1) and normal(1,1):

24
00:01:32,367 --> 00:01:37,227
their dot product1−1 is zero.

25
00:01:37,627 --> 00:01:45,467
Scaling x by two produces tangent(2,−1),
changing the surface slope.

26
00:01:45,727 --> 00:01:52,947
Applying M to the normal gives(2,1), whose dot
with the new tangent is4−1=3 rather than zero.

27
00:01:53,247 --> 00:01:58,487
Normalizing the wrong vector by square root
five leaves dot3

28
00:01:58,727 --> 00:02:01,667
divided by square root five, so its direction
remains wrong.

29
00:02:02,087 --> 00:02:06,387
The inverse transpose scales x by one half,

30
00:02:06,627 --> 00:02:13,147
giving(.5,1) with transformed dot1−1=0.

31
00:02:13,507 --> 00:02:18,587
Final normalization gives
approximately(.447,.894):

32
00:02:18,827 --> 00:02:22,367
first correct the perpendicular direction,
then normalize.

33
00:02:22,450 --> 00:02:26,867
The same perpendicularity condition extends
to3D;

34
00:02:27,207 --> 00:02:31,547
distinguish the linear position transform from
the normal matrix in lighting code.

35
00:02:32,333 --> 00:02:35,573
Move among Megabonk rocks and buildings,

36
00:02:35,933 --> 00:02:39,733
separating faceted rock surfaces from wall
markings.

37
00:02:43,233 --> 00:02:46,553
View changes preserve angular outlines, while
face brightness and

38
00:02:46,873 --> 00:02:52,453
wall markings motivate lighting and
surface-property questions.

39
00:02:55,932 --> 00:02:58,972
The next passage crosses low rocks and sand;

40
00:02:59,312 --> 00:03:03,032
the final section alternately reveals building
front and side faces.

41
00:03:06,531 --> 00:03:09,231
Windows and strip-like marks appear on faces;

42
00:03:09,291 --> 00:03:15,511
we observe their relationship without reading
game UVs or texture files.

43
00:03:19,231 --> 00:03:22,571
Our numerical example preserved normal-tangent
perpendicularity;

44
00:03:23,171 --> 00:03:26,431
next connect separate image coordinates for
patterns.

45
00:03:30,470 --> 00:03:35,950
Correct geometry and normals cannot fix lookup
of the wrong image region;

46
00:03:36,790 --> 00:03:38,950
verify these data roles separately.

47
00:03:39,750 --> 00:03:43,110
Inspect another Sunset Overdrive roof and
wall,

48
00:03:43,390 --> 00:03:47,610
where repeated rectangular windows meet a
sloped roof.

49
00:03:48,220 --> 00:03:52,100
Observe the large outline first, then internal
patterns;

50
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:56,720
abundant detail does not imply one vertex per
detail.

51
00:03:57,430 --> 00:04:01,270
The closer wall shows brick-like patterns and
grids,

52
00:04:01,490 --> 00:04:05,990
motivating correspondence between image
locations and surface locations.

53
00:04:06,540 --> 00:04:10,600
We have not inspected game texture files or
address modes;

54
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:15,500
the visible patterns motivate separate pattern
coordinates.

55
00:04:16,090 --> 00:04:21,010
Our diagram applies an original labeled
texture to a four-vertex

56
00:04:21,210 --> 00:04:25,130
plate and changes its image mapping while
keeping the plate fixed.

57
00:04:25,750 --> 00:04:29,250
A texture stores surface color or other
attributes;

58
00:04:29,610 --> 00:04:34,450
its texels differ from pixels in the final
image.

59
00:04:34,750 --> 00:04:38,530
One texel may affect multiple screen pixels
when magnified,

60
00:04:38,910 --> 00:04:42,350
while multiple texels may contribute to a
smaller screen footprint.

61
00:04:42,420 --> 00:04:47,950
Here u runs right and v down,

62
00:04:47,950 --> 00:04:51,690
with top-left(0,0) and bottom-right(1,1).

63
00:04:51,930 --> 00:04:56,310
This is an explicit example convention; file
row order,

64
00:04:56,310 --> 00:05:00,830
tools and transforms matter, so API names
alone do not decide orientation.

65
00:05:01,370 --> 00:05:05,990
Normalized0-to1 coordinates cover an image
regardless of

66
00:05:06,430 --> 00:05:09,650
whether it is8 by8 or larger, independently
of3D positions.

67
00:05:09,930 --> 00:05:13,890
Planar, cylindrical and spherical mapping
construct the correspondence,

68
00:05:14,450 --> 00:05:19,010
authored in tools or computed procedurally at
vertices.

69
00:05:19,390 --> 00:05:22,550
Interpolate vertex UVs inside faces, retaining
the earlier

70
00:05:22,610 --> 00:05:28,030
perspective-correct treatment while focusing
on the data being interpolated.

71
00:05:28,683 --> 00:05:32,623
Imagine pinning cloth to four plate vertices;

72
00:05:32,623 --> 00:05:36,183
their selected image locations define the
interior correspondence.

73
00:05:36,275 --> 00:05:42,103
Assign(0,0),(1,0),(1,1),(0,1) in order to
cover the

74
00:05:42,283 --> 00:05:44,303
plate with the entire image without moving
vertices.

75
00:05:44,426 --> 00:05:47,283
Restrict both coordinates to.25 through.75,

76
00:05:47,283 --> 00:05:50,083
stretching the central half-width and
half-height over the plate.

77
00:05:50,091 --> 00:05:53,423
Cycling the UV corners rotates the pattern

78
00:05:53,456 --> 00:05:57,603
correspondence rather than rotating the3D
plate.

79
00:05:57,636 --> 00:06:00,423
Using1−u flips the image horizontally while

80
00:06:00,663 --> 00:06:05,183
preserving geometry positions and face
winding.

81
00:06:05,443 --> 00:06:09,643
UVs also apply to multi-triangle meshes,

82
00:06:09,643 --> 00:06:13,083
changing placement and stretching without
changing their geometry.

83
00:06:13,243 --> 00:06:15,703
The cloth analogy explains correspondence,

84
00:06:15,843 --> 00:06:21,063
not physical cloth simulation or guaranteed
area preservation.

85
00:06:21,683 --> 00:06:29,243
Move near a red facade where markings sit
within a larger curved form.

86
00:06:29,695 --> 00:06:33,695
View changes alter the apparent pattern
orientation and size,

87
00:06:33,955 --> 00:06:37,895
reminding us that texture and screen
coordinates differ.

88
00:06:38,367 --> 00:06:42,007
The next roof shows repeated strips and glass
panes;

89
00:06:42,447 --> 00:06:46,827
the container then reveals differently
oriented top and side panels.

90
00:06:47,299 --> 00:06:50,559
We do not read these game UVs; the observed
pattern-surface

91
00:06:50,559 --> 00:06:56,211
relationships motivate our explicitly authored
examples.

92
00:06:56,491 --> 00:07:00,691
As with our plate, a surface can map a whole
image or a portion;

93
00:07:01,051 --> 00:07:05,011
next consider coordinates outside0 through1.

94
00:07:05,331 --> 00:07:09,863
Repeated appearance alone does not prove
repeat addressing:

95
00:07:10,063 --> 00:07:13,983
separate geometry or repeated content in an
image can look similar.

96
00:07:15,033 --> 00:07:17,663
Characters move in Big Walk green room;

97
00:07:17,883 --> 00:07:21,603
locate the black grid panel and circular wall
markings.

98
00:07:24,760 --> 00:07:27,700
Turning views changes the grid apparent angle;

99
00:07:27,920 --> 00:07:32,460
screen locations differ from pattern locations
on a surface.

100
00:07:35,738 --> 00:07:40,858
As characters approach and move sideways,

101
00:07:41,118 --> 00:07:43,798
markings remain associated with their surface
regions, motivating texture correspondence.

102
00:07:46,955 --> 00:07:52,415
Repeated shapes do not prove repeat
addressing;

103
00:07:52,415 --> 00:07:55,155
the repetitions could be contained within the
image itself.

104
00:07:58,392 --> 00:08:03,012
The black grid has not been verified as
triangle topology;

105
00:08:03,312 --> 00:08:07,152
distinguish repeated markings from geometric
connections.

106
00:08:10,409 --> 00:08:15,729
Our next diagram uses one original image with
explicitly chosen UV spans and addressing,

107
00:08:16,109 --> 00:08:19,229
letting us verify the resulting patterns.

108
00:08:22,487 --> 00:08:26,727
Remember to interpolate within the face before
texture lookup;

109
00:08:26,987 --> 00:08:30,247
we also compare the failure of folding vertex
coordinates too early.

110
00:08:31,280 --> 00:08:33,887
UVs can exceed0 through1. Keep vertices and
UVs

111
00:08:34,107 --> 00:08:38,847
identical while comparing repeat and clamp
addressing.

112
00:08:39,207 --> 00:08:42,787
Repeat reuses the image across integer tiles;

113
00:08:42,867 --> 00:08:46,607
use u minus floor u consistently for negative
coordinates.

114
00:08:46,967 --> 00:08:50,027
For1.25 floor is1, giving.25;

115
00:08:50,947 --> 00:08:55,327
for−.75 floor is−1, also giving.25.

116
00:08:55,587 --> 00:08:58,607
Truncating toward zero differs for negative
inputs;

117
00:08:58,607 --> 00:09:03,107
distinguish floor from truncation when
implementing repeat.

118
00:09:03,527 --> 00:09:10,467
Clamp limits below-zero to0 and above-one to1,
extending edge values outward.

119
00:09:10,767 --> 00:09:17,107
Mirrored repeat alternates tile orientation,
and u and v may use separate address modes.

120
00:09:17,487 --> 00:09:22,787
Addressing selects coordinates independently
of filtering, which combines texels;

121
00:09:22,987 --> 00:09:26,447
exact edge behavior also depends on those
sampling conventions.

122
00:09:27,067 --> 00:09:32,507
A0-to2 u edge should display two repeats;

123
00:09:32,787 --> 00:09:38,187
store the unmodified endpoint coordinates0
and2.

124
00:09:38,547 --> 00:09:45,167
Interpolation gives.5 at one quarter and1.5 at
three quarters along the edge.

125
00:09:45,647 --> 00:09:50,527
Applying repeat at lookup maps both positions
to.5,

126
00:09:50,867 --> 00:09:53,587
the same relative location in different tiles.

127
00:09:53,587 --> 00:09:59,067
Wrapping endpoints0 and2 first turns both into
zero,

128
00:09:59,587 --> 00:10:02,847
so interpolation loses the entire0-to2
variation.

129
00:10:03,347 --> 00:10:08,107
The two-repeat span is lost;

130
00:10:08,487 --> 00:10:12,327
interpolate first and apply addressing only at
texture lookup.

131
00:10:12,727 --> 00:10:18,307
Use the preceding perspective-correct UV
interpolation before this step;

132
00:10:18,647 --> 00:10:22,527
perspective correction and repeat order are
separate requirements.

133
00:10:23,233 --> 00:10:27,173
Move among stairs and buildings in Sunset
Overdrive,

134
00:10:27,453 --> 00:10:30,573
first identifying near and distant surface
boundaries.

135
00:10:30,973 --> 00:10:33,853
As roofs and building sides appear, separate
three questions: geometry,

136
00:10:34,193 --> 00:10:37,493
lighting direction and surface pattern.

137
00:10:38,113 --> 00:10:43,573
The following wall has repeated windows and
the roof repeated panels,

138
00:10:43,853 --> 00:10:47,153
which do not reveal vertex counts or address
modes.

139
00:10:47,273 --> 00:10:51,873
Our calculations used indices for vertex
records,

140
00:10:51,873 --> 00:10:56,553
normals for perpendicular directions and UVs
for image correspondence; their roles differ.

141
00:10:56,793 --> 00:11:00,413
When checking a model, inspect silhouettes,

142
00:11:00,533 --> 00:11:05,313
normal boundaries and pattern placement
separately to narrow the source of an error.

143
00:11:05,693 --> 00:11:08,673
Finish with explicitly defined mesh exercises
that verify storage

144
00:11:08,953 --> 00:11:13,613
and calculation order rather than guessing
game internals.

145
00:11:14,233 --> 00:11:21,733
First exercise: four32-byte vertices and
six2-byte indices total140 bytes;

146
00:11:21,913 --> 00:11:24,993
distinguish record count from index count.

147
00:11:25,453 --> 00:11:29,873
Second, split a cube corner shading direction
by duplicating

148
00:11:29,873 --> 00:11:33,913
records and updating the corresponding face
indices.

149
00:11:34,353 --> 00:11:39,593
The transformed tangent(2,−1) and normal(.5,1)
have zero dot product;

150
00:11:39,693 --> 00:11:45,493
verify perpendicularity and unit length after
nonuniform scale.

151
00:11:45,813 --> 00:11:48,453
Third, preserve0-to2 UVs for interpolation,

152
00:11:48,773 --> 00:11:53,973
repeat at lookup and use floor for negative
coordinates.

153
00:11:54,253 --> 00:12:00,393
We separated topology indices, shading normals
and UV correspondence;

154
00:12:00,593 --> 00:12:03,733
equal positions need not share all attributes.

155
00:12:04,173 --> 00:12:11,033
Next we evaluate lighting with these normals
and examine lights and materials;

156
00:12:11,073 --> 00:12:15,373
today surface data supplies those inputs.
